WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.163
Film Courage: What confuses&nbsp;
a new screenwriter the most?

00:00:02.163 --> 00:00:04.860
Daniel Calvisi, Script Doctor, Writing Coach&nbsp;
and Author: I think it's voice on the page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:14.760
It is selecting a word choice that captures tone&nbsp;
and style and develop developing their original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:24.360
voice on the page. It's a big thing, it is not&nbsp;
totally quantifiable. I mean it's you can't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:31.800
teach someone their voice. It's literally which&nbsp;
words do you choose in a description paragraph to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:40.980
capture the moment and I think new writers don't&nbsp;
quite get how important tone is, how important it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.460
is to capture the style and tone of a moment&nbsp;
of an individual scene or even just a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:55.320
of screen time they should think as if they're&nbsp;
watching that movie or TV show at that moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.600
what would the what would the audience feel in&nbsp;
that moment? Then try and recreate that with words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:08.280
on the page. It might apply to paragraph breaks,&nbsp;
punctuation might help, white space versus text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:16.380
everything adds up to creating the tone of that&nbsp;
particular scene and the pace and the style.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.840
You can't fully teach that but I can give them&nbsp;
enough notes and even edits and I can say here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:32.040
how you might rewrite this description maybe if&nbsp;
you were use a word that's a little bit stronger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:39.060
here, this one seems a little flat. I can push&nbsp;
them in that direction and then hopefully they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.760
get into the groove and feel their voice on&nbsp;
the page and it'll get to be second hand.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.560
Film Courage: Do you ever have a writer&nbsp;
think that they have a distinct voice but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.310
it turns out they're really emulating&nbsp;
their favorite author or screenwriter?

00:01:53.310 --> 00:02:01.260
Dan: It happens, it happens. I've had&nbsp;
writers write like really staccato short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:12.840
description lines and it's kind of just too much,&nbsp;
it's too choppy. So the opposite of that is those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.340
big thick description paragraphs you don't want&nbsp;
those either but you do want kind of a balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:26.340
between smaller description paragraph maybe just&nbsp;
one line of description maybe a bigger one use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:34.140
white space It should flow you don't want too&nbsp;
long of monologues in your dialogue it is hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:42.780
to quantify but the page a good comedy page feels&nbsp;
different and maybe even looks different than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:50.400
good biopic page what are the biggest mistakes&nbsp;
you see made over and over again in screenplays

00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:00.660
I would say not capturing tone the tone of&nbsp;
the scene in both description and dialogue

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.020
having characters not have&nbsp;
distinctive voices in their dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.400
too long pacing's too slow on the nose dialogue

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.980
not getting us into the story as fast as possible&nbsp;
because when that reader starts your script from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:29.100
the get-go they want to know what the story is&nbsp;
what what is the concept what is the engine what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:36.780
am I watching and it doesn't mean you have to&nbsp;
launch the engine at the bottom of page one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:45.900
course you can let it play out and take some time&nbsp;
and surprise us but just know that you can't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:54.300
30 pages of just set up before you even introduce&nbsp;
any of the main engines or main story elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.720
we're looking for what makes this unique and&nbsp;
what is the journey we're going to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.460
can you explain what engine means an engine is&nbsp;
a character pursuing a goal against obstacles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:16.800
and it's preceded usually by a catalyst so&nbsp;
they're thrown into a situation in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.720
they're now pursuing a goal and there&nbsp;
are antagonistic forces against them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:30.480
it usually your goals internal and external&nbsp;
those are both engines so will the hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:37.320
defeat the villain that's your external engine&nbsp;
they're they're fighting to catch the villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.360
and the villain is fighting against them or&nbsp;
there's other factors in the way obstacles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:48.780
and then external will they reunite with&nbsp;
their spouse okay so that's more of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:56.340
emotional maybe romantic engine but it's still&nbsp;
an engine they want to win back their spouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:03.180
and there are obstacles in between so you can&nbsp;
call that an engine what's driving the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:14.700
with a TV series I like to define the compelling&nbsp;
crisis which is like the core of the drama it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:23.100
the core problem that that show is showcasing and&nbsp;
it's usually an engine it's will this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.900
achieve this against these obstacles&nbsp;
it's kind of a mini log line if you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:37.980
so you want to know what that core crux problem&nbsp;
is you probably want one in in a in a pilot you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.140
want an A and A-B story definitely you might have&nbsp;
a C and a D story, you probably don't want to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.800
also an E, F, G, H, I, J story you don't&nbsp;
want to have too many engines going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:57.900
unless it's a multi-character piece like White&nbsp;
Lotus like the White Lotus but even there I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:04.140
think you really only have four separate engines&nbsp;
four separate camps with the different characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.420
I think in season two maybe they added there were&nbsp;
more characters there was more going on but that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:19.140
a specific type of story and it's an ensemble&nbsp;
piece it's a multi-character and those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:25.260
tougher to write but that is a route you could go&nbsp;
can we define what tone is can we give an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:36.960
tone in screenplays is really word choice to&nbsp;
create the feeling of a scene and it's hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:44.820
quantify because there's a billion words you could&nbsp;
use to capture a certain scene and that's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:51.960
the special sauce that the writer brings what&nbsp;
words punctuation black versus white space on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.960 --> 00:07:00.240
the page are they going to use to capture that&nbsp;
tone but if you think of I mean so something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:07.440
like get out which has a creepy scary tone that's&nbsp;
probably going to look different on the page than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:19.680
as good as it gets and it's hard it depends on&nbsp;
the example of course but I'm always trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

